<meta type=article>
  <title | Unclosed-tag-at-EOF fixture>
  <author | Test Author>
</meta>

<section | Document body before the unclosed tag>

The aside below opens but never closes. Under Option A's EOF-only
terminator rule, the tag consumes content all the way to EOF and
produces a visible `acadamarkTagError` at its opening position. The
content past the unclosed tag becomes part of the error node's
source as best-effort content. The author sees the error marker and
the conspicuously missing downstream content — itself a signal that
the document has an unclosed tag.

This fixture pins the EOF-terminator half of Option A against
regression. ML-8 in `remark-acadamark/test/test.js` covers the
EOF-without-closer → `acadamarkTagError` path at the parser level;
this fixture covers the visible-rendering end-to-end.

<aside | This aside opens with a pipe and never closes.

Author-intended body text that follows, never visible as body in
the rendered output — by Option A's EOF-only design, this content
is swallowed into the error node's source. The bounded cost is
acceptable: the error renders visibly at the open position, and
the missing post-tag content is itself a strong author signal.
